        <p>*Note: Health risks may begin at lower BMI levels in Asian populations (&#x02265;23 kg/m&#x000b2; for increased risk, &#x02265;25 kg/m&#x000b2; for high risk) due to different body composition patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-179671.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Educational and socioeconomic gradients</bold>
</p>
        <p>A clear&#x000a0;inverse relationship&#x000a0;exists between educational attainment and obesity prevalence, demonstrating the powerful influence of social determinants of health on weight status (see <bold>Table 3</bold>).</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-179671.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 3. Obesity Prevalence by Educational Attainment</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
